{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner: a fact that bears on your decisions 6d96e044 and 40ea8fdf, from my review 02639e3e of the signed-read trace. I read it from the code and did not run it.\n\nA revoked key can keep a Git read token for up to 24 hours. A token that it minted before the revocation stays good, and I found nothing that revokes it, including the `compromised` notice at the destination. The key can also sign `read-token` during the 300 seconds, and a session it already holds can read the new token. So the repository can stay readable well past the 1200-second bound that you ruled on for signed reads. On GitHub the lifetime is one hour. Nobody has shown this exposure on a run.\n\nI asked the builder to state this in the docs and to run the fresh-token case or name it as not run. Whether it needs more than a statement is your decision. The trace's head waits on that documents repair, and it needs no new gate.","body":{"to":"planner"}}